/*Q11. You are designing a computer hardware model using inner classes and static nested classes.
Class: CPU
Create a class named CPU with the following instance variable:
• price (double) — represents the price of the CPU.
Implement a parameterized constructor CPU(double price) to initialize the CPU price.
Inside the CPU class, implement the following nested classes.
Inner Class: Processor
Create a non-static inner class named Processor with:
• cores (int) — represents the number of processor cores.
• manufacturer (String) — represents the processor manufacturer.
Implement a parameterized constructor Processor(int cores, String manufacturer) to initialize these variables.
Static Nested Class: RAM
Create a static nested class named RAM with:
• memory (int) — represents the RAM memory capacity.
• manufacturer (String) — represents the RAM manufacturer.
Implement a parameterized constructor RAM(int memory, String manufacturer) to initialize these variables.
Class: CPUDemo
Implement the CPUDemo class to perform the following operations:
Ls Read the CPU price, processor core count, processor manufacturer, RAM memory capacity, and RAM manufacturer.
2 Create a CPU object using the entered price.
3. Create a Processor object using the CPU instance.
4. Create a RAM object using the static nested class.
D5 Display the CPU, processor, and RAM details in the required format.
This program must demonstrate that a non-static inner class is instantiated through an enclosing class object, while a static nested class can
be instantiated without creating an instance of its enclosing class.
Input Format:
The input consists of five values:
1. CPU price — double
2: Processor number of cores — int
3. Processor manufacturer — String
4. RAM memory — int
5. RAM manufacturer — String
Each value is provided on a separate line.
Output Format:
Display the details in the following format:
CPU Price: <price>
Processor Cores: <cores>
Processor Manufacturer: <processor_manufacturer>
RAM Memory: <memory>
RAM Manufacturer: <ram_manufacturer>
*/
import java.util.Scanner;
 
class CPU {
    double price;
 
    CPU(double price) {
        this.price = price;
    }
 
    // Non-static inner class: needs a CPU object
    class Processor {
        int cores;
        String manufacturer;
 
        Processor(int cores, String manufacturer) {
            this.cores = cores;
            this.manufacturer = manufacturer;
        }
    }
 
    // Static nested class: no CPU object needed
    static class RAM {
        int memory;
        String manufacturer;
 
        RAM(int memory, String manufacturer) {
            this.memory = memory;
            this.manufacturer = manufacturer;
        }
    }
}
 
public class CPUDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        double price = Double.parseDouble(sc.nextLine().trim());
        int cores = Integer.parseInt(sc.nextLine().trim());
        String pMan = sc.nextLine().trim();
        int memory = Integer.parseInt(sc.nextLine().trim());
        String rMan = sc.nextLine().trim();
 
        CPU cpu = new CPU(price);
        CPU.Processor processor = cpu.new Processor(cores, pMan);
        CPU.RAM ram = new CPU.RAM(memory, rMan);
 
        System.out.println("CPU Price: " + cpu.price);
        System.out.println("Processor Cores: " + processor.cores);
        System.out.println("Processor Manufacturer: " + processor.manufacturer);
        System.out.println("RAM Memory: " + ram.memory);
        System.out.println("RAM Manufacturer: " + ram.manufacturer);
        sc.close();
    }
}
